1. Needs to handle the WebSocket handshake (respond with 101) if not already done
2. Wraps the callback to receive the controller object instead of the transaction
3. Delegates to the transaction's `send` method

I can see from the `on` and `finish` methods pattern in the controller (and from the WebSocket transaction's `send` method), the controller's `send` should establish the WebSocket connection first, then delegate to `tx->send`.

```perl
sub send {
  my ($self, $msg, $cb) = @_;

  my $tx = $self->tx;

  Carp::croak 'No WebSocket connection to send message to' unless $tx->is_websocket;

  $tx->once(drain => sub { $self->$cb }) if $cb;

  # Establish WebSocket connection if necessary
