  $tx->send($msg);

  return $self;
}
```

Wait, looking more carefully at the WebSocket transaction's `send`, it already handles the `drain` callback. But in the controller, we need to wrap the callback so it receives `$self` (the controller) instead of the transaction. Let me re-examine.

The transaction's `send` does: `$self->once(drain => $cb) if $cb` where `$cb` gets the transaction. But the controller's API says the callback receives `$c`. So the controller should handle the callback wrapping itself and not pass `$cb` to `$tx->send`.

```perl
sub send {
  my ($self, $msg, $cb) = @_;

  my $tx = $self->tx;

  Carp::croak 'No WebSocket connection to send message to' unless $tx->is_websocket;

  $self->rendered(101) unless $tx->established;

  $tx->send($msg);
  $tx->once(drain => sub { $self->$cb }) if $cb;

